POPEN(3)                       P32W Unix                        POPEN(3)

NAME
       popen, pclose - a pipe to or from a command

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       FILE *popen(const char *cmd, const char *mode);
       int pclose(FILE *f);

DESCRIPTION
       popen runs cmd as /bin/sh -c cmd and returns a stream joined
       to it by a pipe. With mode "r" the stream reads what the
       command writes on its standard output; with "w" what is
       written to the stream becomes the command's standard input.
       The command runs alongside the program.

       pclose closes the stream (so a command reading it sees the
       end of its input) and waits for the command to finish.

       Several popen streams can be open at once and closed in any
       order: a command does not inherit the other streams' pipes,
       and pclose keeps the status of a command that ends while it
       waits for another, for its own pclose.

RETURN VALUE
       popen returns the stream, or NULL if the mode is not r or w,
       or a pipe or process could not be made. pclose returns the
       command's status as wait(2) gives it (WIFEXITED,
       WEXITSTATUS, WTERMSIG), or -1 if f did not come from popen.
       An exit status of 127 means the shell could not be run.

EXAMPLES
       FILE *p = popen("ls /bin", "r");
       char line[100];
       while (fgets(line, sizeof line, p))
           ...
       if (WEXITSTATUS(pclose(p)) != 0)
           ...

SEE ALSO
       system(3), sh(1), pipe(2), fork(2), wait(2)
